And this is one of those where had this happened, had this research happened before we did that episode, it would have won 100 percent to be in that episode because it's awesome.

53
00:05:01,966 --> 00:05:10,189
Excellent. This is research by Natalie Jones et al. in OICOS and the article is also by Natalie Jones in The Conversation.

54
00:05:10,729 --> 00:05:12,050
Link in the blog post.

55
00:05:12,544 --> 00:05:27,948
Dormancy, as we discussed in that episode, is a behavior and condition that many organisms can enact and go into where it is lowered activity, lower typically metabolic rates and body temperature.

56
00:05:28,608 --> 00:05:34,850
It's good for surviving winter. It's good for getting through tough times and droughts and stuff.

57
00:05:34,870 --> 00:05:35,670
The dry season.

58
00:05:35,704 --> 00:05:43,527
Yeah, things where being active might be too taxing for what the environment can currently provide.

59
00:05:44,364 --> 00:06:07,073
It has been hypothesized that this could help animals in surviving extinction events potentially when the ecosystem is in flux, but also that it might be able to help a wider variety of species coexist by lowering the taxation on the environment and making space for more individuals.

60
00:06:07,190 --> 00:06:13,792
Yeah, if each species is only needing resources for like 50 percent of the time, you can have twice as many species.

61
00:06:13,872 --> 00:06:24,395
Yeah, and even if there's like just one that practicing dormancy, maybe that can make enough space for another that would have been pushed out by competition one way or the other.

62
00:06:25,005 --> 00:06:31,987
This is a super cool question, but hasn't been tested and hasn't been looked at in too much detail.

63
00:06:32,768 --> 00:06:40,730
So they wanted to look at this, both these aspects of this question of does this help animals survive in these two scenarios?

64
00:06:41,266 --> 00:06:56,550
And they looked at it with nematodes, specifically, they're looking at the form of dormancy, which is diapause, which is the typical one you think of, bear hibernation and stuff like that, that periodic lowered activity that's typically in some sort of cycle.

65
00:06:57,128 --> 00:07:15,254
They looked at the nematode species Canorhabditis elegans. This is a kind of nematode worm found very commonly in the soil. Nothing super crazy about it. But the genetic pathway that affects dormancy is very well understood in these worms, which means it can be modified.

66
00:07:15,707 --> 00:07:26,869
So they looked at four groups of worms, the first, which was genetically more inclined to enter dormancy, second, less inclined to enter dormancy, third, unable to enter dormancy.

67
00:07:27,369 --> 00:07:32,870
And the fourth was a wild type, which has a medium propensity to dormancy.

68
00:07:33,565 --> 00:08:02,952
They then created a experimental situation where these groups were in a scenario where they had to compete over food in the environment, along with another worm, which was Canorhabditis briggsae, to try to see which varying situation of dormancy behavior would how would that affect the ability for these different organisms to coexist or not coexist?

69
00:08:03,609 --> 00:08:22,293
In a competitive scenario. Interesting. So they have known amounts and then a natural amount and a separate competitor. Now we can look at does dormancy increase or decrease or affect how well organisms can coexist in a competitive environment?

70
00:08:22,791 --> 00:08:36,014
They found that when the species are more inclined to dormancy, competing species were able to coexist under a wider range of environmental conditions. They simulate that with different temperatures in the situations.

71
00:08:36,450 --> 00:08:51,774
So I think they had multiple ones set at different temperatures. Through these varying environmental conditions, species with a higher investment in dormancy, so the ones that tended to do it more often, were able to coexist with the competitor over a wider range of temperatures.

72
00:08:52,787 --> 00:09:03,489
So this means that this up-regulating of dormancy does seem to increase the number of species that can exist, coexist in a single environment.

73
00:09:03,709 --> 00:09:11,390
Yeah, well, and in particular, that it widens the range of habitats that they can coexist.

74
00:09:11,485 --> 00:09:33,951
Yes, this is one of the first experimental confirmations of this hypothesis, which is very cool. So it does indeed seem that dormancy likely has an effect on population dynamics within an ecosystem, not just of the dormant behaving creature, but of its potential rivals.

75
00:09:34,510 --> 00:09:56,916
They noted that this was predicted. This is not a surprising result. This is exactly what we expected based off of that hypothesis. But it also can be used to further explore the role of dormancy in species persistence and in other situations, and might be a good next step to try and answer the question of does it help them survive extinctions?

76
00:09:57,169 --> 00:10:06,391
Yeah, and as always comes up, is a very important question right now, while many of them may be facing the modern extinction that many animals are facing.

