My first news is about a new species of ancient amphibians from the tropical regions of Pangaea with some thematic consistency to the thing you were just talking about.

81
00:10:35,086 --> 00:10:49,229
This is research by Calvin Soh et al in Proceedings of the Royal Society Bee. We will link to a press release in the blog post on phys.org from the Field Museum. So Pangaea was a weird place.

82
00:10:49,629 --> 00:10:49,949
A bit.

83
00:10:50,324 --> 00:11:18,069
Pangaea, we talked about this in episode 141 about supercontinents, when all the continents landmasses were mostly all together. During times like the late Triassic period, which is the time in question here, there is evidence from rock formations and also climate models that Pangaea had weird climate conditions going on, particularly very extreme seasonality across the length of the continent,

84
00:11:18,506 --> 00:11:38,630
particularly in the tropics because of the full extent of the landmass and the way that climate and weather conditions interact over large landmasses and large water masses, there are thought to have been very extreme seasonal shifts, particularly in places like the tropics.

85
00:11:39,128 --> 00:12:06,773
Researchers in the past have introduced the term megamonsoons to describe this sort of radical seasonality. Part of the support for this is that ecosystems in the northern parts of Pangaea and in the southern parts of Pangaea in the fossil record are very different from each other, which suggests that not a lot of species had the ability to go through the middle, that that tropical area was a barrier.

86
00:12:07,249 --> 00:12:29,933
But we don't know a lot about the tropical habitats of Pangaea because we don't have a lot of fossil evidence from there. This study describes some fossils of temnospondyls, ancient amphibians, from the Gelma Formation in Wyoming, late Triassic, about 230 million years old, and at the time was the tropics.

87
00:12:30,270 --> 00:12:50,474
This discovery began, as the press release details, with the discovery of infilled chambers of sediment within these rock formations, which were little cylindrical tunnels, short tunnels, filled with sediment, which were interpreted as burrows.

88
00:12:50,510 --> 00:13:04,213
These were burrows that got filled in later on. These little burrows were up to a foot long. When they got to excavating, this burrow hypothesis was confirmed with the discovery of bones inside of them.

89
00:13:04,227 --> 00:13:21,051
Now eat the burrower! The burrowers, lots of little skulls and other bones of these small amphibians. These are a new species, new genus and species, in fact, named Ninambihan dukuduka, which is a name that is derived from the Shoshone language.

90
00:13:21,627 --> 00:13:36,710
They identified a total of around 80 burrows in this formation, most of them with fossils inside. They noted there are no complete skeletons, but lots of bits and pieces that give us a pretty good idea of the whole body of the species.

91
00:13:37,628 --> 00:14:02,893
They estimate that in total, these creatures might have been about a foot long, about as long as those burrows were. Just enough to hide, not enough to run laps or anything. Yes. They noted that they also have long bodies with relatively small arms and sort of scoop-shaped skulls, which we see in a lot of modern animals that burrow.

92
00:14:03,009 --> 00:14:18,932
Yeah, hog-nosed amphibians. Yes. We see this in some burrowing lizards that have reduced arms but dig with their face. In the study, they specifically compare them to a number of modern salamanders and fish that burrow.

93
00:14:19,409 --> 00:14:34,172
Oh yeah. Like lungfish and stuff that make burrows, they're not digging with their claws, they're scooping dirt out of the way with their face. That's a good point because typically when I think of burrow-making fish, I think of marine ones and they are moving sand with their mouth usually.

94
00:14:34,267 --> 00:14:54,632
Yeah. But like in lungfish, they're just having to kind of push into the mud. Yeah. So that makes sense. Based on the features of these temnospondyls, they seem to be closely related to a general group that have also been inferred as burrowers. So this seems to be a lineage of these ancient amphibians that were burrowing animals.

95
00:14:55,389 --> 00:15:09,872
The burrows themselves are within what these sediments used to be were point bar deposits in river systems. But specifically, river systems that show evidence of cycling dry and wet conditions.

96
00:15:10,228 --> 00:15:27,952
Yeah. This is that extreme seasonality. There would have been a dry season and a wet season. So the interpretation here, what appears to have been captured at this site, is that these amphibians were likely spending the dry season underground.

97
00:15:28,109 --> 00:15:41,492
Yes. Because they're amphibians, they need water. Everything needs water. But you know, when the water leaves, when the rains go away and it's the dry season, they burrow down and hunker down until the rains return.

98
00:15:42,068 --> 00:16:02,733
Whether they were hibernating or just waiting, obviously we can't say, but they were doing the same thing that a lot of modern animals will use their dormant periods to do, waiting around. This is interesting. They note that probably these became fossilized after the river system changed.

99
00:16:02,908 --> 00:16:12,790
That one year the rains didn't return or at least the water didn't return to the river system and so they never got the chance to come back out.

100
00:16:13,030 --> 00:16:26,593
Which happens all the time. Absolutely. If a rock falls and blocks an area or the landscape shifts, suddenly the river is going east instead of west and a whole ecosystem can just be deleted.

101
00:16:27,628 --> 00:16:38,171
So these appear to have been left behind by the changing landscape. But fortunately, so much, such cool data for us. Yeah, once again, the more tragedy, the better for fossils.

102
00:16:39,408 --> 00:16:56,753
This is interesting, very exciting for lots of reasons, but on top of all of them, this burrowing habit seems to be the first direct evidence of adaptations to the tropical conditions of Pangaea.

103
00:16:57,207 --> 00:17:09,870
Oh, that makes sense. Yeah, that would be, wouldn't it? In order to survive, these animals would have had to burrow like this. Yes. Oh, that's very cool.

104
00:17:10,070 --> 00:17:19,413
It's neat because this behavior is pretty common in a lot of amphibians today that live in dry habitats. Oh, sure. There's toads that do it. There's salamanders that do it.

105
00:17:19,427 --> 00:17:31,870
I think there are Sicilians that are known to like get down and keep themselves moist. But yeah, amphibians do this. I had never thought about ancient amphibians doing it just because it's so easy to not think of those sort of things.

106
00:17:32,306 --> 00:17:48,970
So that's delightful. But that's a very cool point that this is because of the time they're living, the reason they have to deal with that isn't just because they live in a desert. It's because the continents are fundamentally different.

107
00:17:49,007 --> 00:18:07,292
Yeah, this might be their adaptation for surviving the mega monsoon cycle of Pangaea, which is extra fun to find it. We specifically, you and I, so often talk about amphibians as these like fragile little creatures. Right. Because in many ways they are.

108
00:18:07,368 --> 00:18:26,372
Yes. Compared to like the tough skin of a lizard. Absolutely. They are very sensitive. Amphibians today are ecosystem indicators because they're so sensitive to changes in their environment that, yeah, if the water gets a little polluted, then all your frogs start to die because they're so sensitive to that.

109
00:18:26,487 --> 00:18:40,890
Yes. But they're also extraordinarily good at surviving. Yeah. Well, they're also very sci-fi creatures in that you can chop the arms off of many of them. They'll grow a back. Yeah. They can just go into a state of suspended animation.

110
00:18:40,910 --> 00:19:00,453
Yeah. Some of them can freeze themselves. So yeah, the fact that temnospondyls were doing this back in the Triassic, very cool to know. I wonder how extreme their dormancy might have been. Like, was the mega monsoon cycle consistent year to year or was it like, hey, it might be back in three years?

111
00:19:01,329 --> 00:19:19,313
Sure. Sure. Well, like I'm sure there would have been climatic fluctuations. Like we had the El Nino cycles today where climate sort of shifts on multi-year patterns. Well, and I know that happens with a lot of deserts today where it's like the rains usually come every two and a half years where it's not a yearly cycle.

112
00:19:19,350 --> 00:19:36,194
It's like every few years we get a heavy rain. We might get little sprinkles in between. Yeah. But the cycle is not on a yearly pattern. Would Pangaea be on something extreme like that? And would they have been able to, were they adapted to that?

113
00:19:36,588 --> 00:19:45,910
Yeah. How extreme were their adaptations to this extreme continent? Well, speaking of things that can regenerate limbs, there's a reason I brought that up as an example. Absolutely.

114
00:19:46,310 --> 00:19:56,712
For brittle stars, this is research into the genome of brittle star regeneration and what it might tell us about the evolution of regenerating body parts.

115
00:19:57,867 --> 00:20:15,912
This is research by Elise Perry et al in Nature, Ecology and Evolution. And the article is by Sneha Kedkar in The Scientist. This study is mostly looking at that concept of regeneration. So there are tons of animals with the ability to regrow portions of their body.

116
00:20:16,068 --> 00:20:29,652
I mean, we can do that to an extent. Exactly. There are also many, they noted that there's at least a species in almost every major group that can regrow a body part, like a whole limb or a digit or a tail.

117
00:20:30,372 --> 00:20:39,514
But it's unclear whether this ability is a shared thing across those groups of life or individual occurrences of this ability.

118
00:20:40,167 --> 00:20:50,770
So they wanted to try to look at that from a genomic standpoint. They looked at brittle stars, which are echinoderms, they're in the same group as sea stars, sea urchins, sea cucumbers.

119
00:20:51,090 --> 00:21:00,012
Brittle stars famously are typically five pointed, like most sea stars you think of. They're like slender sea stars. And flexible armed.

120
00:21:00,107 --> 00:21:13,371
So they don't use the tube feet to get around the same way. They don't do that hovering movement like a hockey puck that most sea stars you think of do. They will actually move their arms to climb and navigate, more like an octopus.

121
00:21:13,947 --> 00:21:26,850
They are famously regenerative, as are most echinoderms. They noted that in the article, I think that they can regrow their arms in a matter of months, which is very fast, very fast regeneration.

122
00:21:27,410 --> 00:21:38,153
They wanted to get a look at the genetics that were involved in their regeneration. So they collected hundreds of brittle stars from the Fjord in Sweden and brought them back to sequence their DNA.

123
00:21:38,167 --> 00:21:50,670
This is specifically a species that is Amphira filiformes. They extracted and sequenced the DNA, assembled a chromosome scale genome, which had not been done yet for these brittle stars.

124
00:21:50,730 --> 00:22:01,773
So just an addition to science right there. They then mapped, firstly, how brittle stars evolved since they diverged from other echinoderms about 500 million years ago.

125
00:22:02,209 --> 00:22:19,834
Compared their genomes to other echinoderms, sea urchins, sea stars, and sea cucumbers, and found that the brittle star genome had undergone more major genetic changes than the other groups, with a lot of shuffling and moving around of genes across the chromosomes.

126
00:22:20,470 --> 00:22:27,272
They said there was some similarity to sea urchins, but that they showed more than the other groups, a higher amount of the shuffling.

127
00:22:27,892 --> 00:22:34,535
And specifically, the part that caught their attention in this regard is that they had a lot of rearranged genes in their Hox cluster.

128
00:22:35,906 --> 00:22:46,690
Hox genes are famous for being a group of genes that are typically in the same order and on the same chromosomes across basically all animals.

129
00:22:47,030 --> 00:22:50,411
And they're important for the development of the body plan.

130
00:22:50,446 --> 00:23:03,090
Yes, they code for key things like limb goes here, ear goes here, famous examples of fly and mouse Hox genes being compatible to each other for coding body parts.

131
00:23:03,950 --> 00:23:13,594
The fact that brittle stars shuffled their Hox genes is weird. That is a very unusual, we don't see that happen very often, that's a very unusual thing.

132
00:23:14,127 --> 00:23:19,748
They then wanted to look into how these genes were interacting with regeneration.

133
00:23:20,148 --> 00:23:30,591
So they took a bunch of brittle sea stars, almost 3500 animals, and cut an arm off, because these are in kinoderms and you can do that and they will be perfectly fine.

134
00:23:31,051 --> 00:23:33,992
In fact, if you cut too much arm off, you will get two of them.

135
00:23:34,766 --> 00:23:48,811
They then identified the gene expression, so which genes were being turned on and activated during regeneration to see what genes were being used at which steps of regenerating an arm.

136
00:23:49,147 --> 00:24:03,832
They found that early on in generation, genes involved in wound response were more active, including immune reactions and cell migration function, so moving cells to the site.

137
00:24:04,512 --> 00:24:09,954
And then in later stages, genes associated with tissue differentiation and limb shaping.

138
00:24:10,807 --> 00:24:16,529
So getting the tissue to now become the different parts of the arm and taking the right shape of the arm.

139
00:24:17,129 --> 00:24:24,672
They then, now that they knew which genes were being turned on for this process, zoomed in on them and looked at the ancestry of those genes.

140
00:24:25,326 --> 00:24:37,230
They compared them to genes in other limb regenerating animals, specifically they wanted to look at both invertebrate and vertebrate, so they looked at axolotls and a specific amphipod crustacean that can regenerate.

141
00:24:37,690 --> 00:24:48,894
And found that all three animals expressed similar genes during limb regeneration, which is potentially strong support that it is an ancestral feature genetically.

142
00:24:49,246 --> 00:24:57,308
Or at the very least that these, if you're going to evolve regenerative capabilities, these are the genes primed to let you do that.

143
00:24:57,468 --> 00:25:05,190
Which would make it also an extreme case of convergent evolution if it is individually evolved, or it might be a mix of the two.

144
00:25:06,090 --> 00:25:10,231
Some might be sharing an ancestral origin while another came to it separately.

145
00:25:10,564 --> 00:25:31,170
This is definitely the first step in investigating this. They note that this is a proof of concept that ankytonurms are a very useful group to look at when trying to understand the ancestry and potential long history of certain gene expression between vertebrates and invertebrates due to their position on the animal tree.

146
00:25:31,708 --> 00:25:45,111
Very cool. It would make lots of intuitive sense to me if regenerative capabilities like this are a deep ancestral feature among animals, because simpler animals seem to have a much easier time doing it.

147
00:25:45,190 --> 00:26:05,815
Yes. A simpler body plan, a lower number of cell types, like sponges, which basically are all regeneration. Yes. And as more and more complex bodies with more differentiated tissues developed in various lineages that you might lose the ability to regenerate because now it's just too hard to do.

