I believe the Drakensberg group is named after the Drakensberg Mountains, which are down there in that region, or at least they're named for the same person or thing or whatever.

289
00:53:45,786 --> 00:53:46,486
From Kim Possible.

290
00:53:46,966 --> 00:54:00,650
The Drakensberg group is also early Jurassic, up to around 180 million years old, so a good 120 million years after those glaciations that are recorded at the bottom of the Karu Supergroup.

291
00:54:01,166 --> 00:54:10,529
These uppermost sediments are also lake and river sediments, lots of fossils of plants and animals, trackways, all sorts of good stuff like that.

292
00:54:11,008 --> 00:54:26,974
These fossiliferous sediments in these uppermost geologic formations of the Karu Supergroup are interbedded, which means alternating layers, with massive layers of basalt.

293
00:54:29,089 --> 00:54:38,912
Massive layers of basalt that repeat in multiple layers and cover a vast area of geologic space.

294
00:54:39,432 --> 00:54:43,713
We have a term for that. It is a term we have talked about quite a bit.

295
00:54:44,133 --> 00:54:54,496
The Karu Supergroup is capped on top by the volcanic deposits of the Karu Large Igneous Province.

296
00:54:54,785 --> 00:54:59,987
But surely the Karu has everything else. Surely it has to have one of these.

297
00:55:00,007 --> 00:55:06,069
You can't cover 100 million years of Earth history without capturing at least one large igneous province.

298
00:55:06,089 --> 00:55:09,810
If you cast that wide a net, you've got to get one.

299
00:55:09,890 --> 00:55:17,212
This is actually more typically referred to by its group name of the Karu Farrar Large Igneous Province.

300
00:55:17,612 --> 00:55:18,573
I might have heard of that.

301
00:55:18,824 --> 00:55:21,125
Yeah, we've talked about that. That's come up on the podcast before.

302
00:55:21,145 --> 00:55:39,613
Large Igneous Province, if this is the first episode that you've heard us mention it, are vast geological regions of igneous rock layers, typically basalt, which poured out of massive volcanic events.

303
00:55:40,267 --> 00:55:51,371
Usually these are repeated, relatively slow, but very extensive eruptions of basaltic lava.

304
00:55:51,871 --> 00:55:56,673
They are often associated with the splitting of land masses.

305
00:55:57,066 --> 00:56:11,931
As the continent splits apart and the land within that rifting zone becomes thinner, the crust thins, allowing volcanic material to come up from the mantle in that lower pressure zone.

306
00:56:12,531 --> 00:56:18,553
Fast forward many millions of years and what's left is an enormous sequence of volcanic igneous rock.

307
00:56:18,987 --> 00:56:30,851
This is a large igneous province and indeed the rifting that was happening that led to the formation of the Karu Farrar Large Igneous Province is because Pangaea is breaking up at this time.

308
00:56:31,091 --> 00:56:37,313
The Karu Supergroup starts as Pangaea is coming together and ends as Pangaea is breaking apart.

309
00:56:37,593 --> 00:56:40,034
Pretty awesome in caps on either side.

310
00:56:40,546 --> 00:56:54,352
Together the Karu Farrar Large Igneous Province is one of the world's biggest large igneous provinces. The Karu part of it, which is the slightly earlier sequence of eruptions, is present within the Karu Supergroup.

311
00:56:54,872 --> 00:57:00,154
Also there are bits of it farther north in Africa and a bit of it in Antarctica.

312
00:57:00,594 --> 00:57:01,235
Cool!

313
00:57:01,255 --> 00:57:03,676
Because continents were splitting apart at this time.

314
00:57:03,736 --> 00:57:03,956
Yes.

315
00:57:04,502 --> 00:57:09,944
Slightly later, the Farrar Large Igneous Province is present in Antarctica and Tasmania.

316
00:57:10,345 --> 00:57:10,645
Nice.

317
00:57:11,205 --> 00:57:23,210
These were, as seems to be always the case with large igneous provinces, major drivers of environmental change, climate changes, there's a lot of gases and stuff being pumped up into the atmosphere.

318
00:57:23,726 --> 00:57:39,213
These large igneous province events are associated with the Taurasian Oceanic Anoxic Event, a period of widespread low oxygen levels within the ocean, disruptions to the carbon cycle.

319
00:57:39,253 --> 00:57:46,456
This was a real environmental catastrophe kicked off, it seems, by these large igneous province eruptions.

320
00:57:46,989 --> 00:57:54,251
Whether or not these eruptions are themselves actually linked to the Pleinsbachian-Taurasian mass extinction seems to be somewhat debated.

321
00:57:54,431 --> 00:57:55,392
Yeah, yep, yep.

322
00:57:55,783 --> 00:58:14,109
So the Drakensberg Group, these geologic formations way up at the top of the Karu Supergroup, is a record of these diverse ecosystems, dinosaurs and early mammals and plants and stuff, that were repeatedly and consistently covered in lava.

323
00:58:14,389 --> 00:58:17,309
Because the Karu just can't help but continuing to be hardcore.

324
00:58:17,428 --> 00:58:30,692
It is a very cool... well, and there's a lot of research that has gone into this section of the Karu Supergroup looking at what impact did these volcanic eruptions have on this region and the ecosystems that live there.

325
00:58:31,205 --> 00:58:52,251
One of the papers that I read noted that there has been some evidence of shifting north and westward away from the eruptions, that we see this sort of over time migration of various organisms that lived in here, basically escaping from this region that keeps getting covered up in lava.

326
00:58:52,351 --> 00:58:54,872
Yeah, makes sense. Very cool.

327
00:58:55,429 --> 00:59:06,413
As I mentioned earlier, the reason that this area starts collecting sediments is because it is a basin that forms in the sort of shadow of the Cape Fold Mountains.

328
00:59:07,207 --> 00:59:22,734
After this portion of the Karu Supergroup, after these volcanic eruptions, as Pangaea is breaking up, this region stops collecting more sediment than is later eroded over time.

329
00:59:23,114 --> 00:59:23,875
It fills up.

330
00:59:24,327 --> 00:59:33,591
One of the papers that I read actually noted evidence that material was deposited here into the mid-Jurassic but was later eroded away.

331
00:59:33,671 --> 00:59:34,491
Yeah, exactly.

332
00:59:34,551 --> 00:59:42,634
So you get a continuous sequence of geologic formations like this in a place where deposition is outpacing erosion.

333
00:59:42,774 --> 00:59:43,034
Yes.

334
00:59:43,584 --> 00:59:50,667
So your rock layers and sediments can continue to build up and continue to build up without being significantly removed over time.

335
00:59:51,287 --> 00:59:57,249
Once that balance shifts, and even if it doesn't shift right away but later something else happens.

336
00:59:57,767 --> 01:00:09,072
In this region, for example, I mentioned that especially the younger sediments are at much higher elevation, which makes them more susceptible to weathering and erosion from wind and rain and such.

337
01:00:09,565 --> 01:00:23,529
For whatever confluence of reasons that it is, this uppermost early Jurassic sediments routinely covered in lava from a large igneous province is where the Karu Supergroup officially ends.

338
01:00:23,549 --> 01:00:25,870
It is the top of this geologic sequence.

339
01:00:26,530 --> 01:00:27,950
Pretty good.

340
01:00:28,730 --> 01:00:34,712
It's a very, there's a whole saga recorded within this sequence of geology formations.

341
01:00:34,865 --> 01:00:47,210
Well, and it's also like we've got, we're getting to see the story of this section of Earth through all that time, which is scientifically, you know, incalculably useful.

342
01:00:47,987 --> 01:01:04,172
Also, from just a human perspective, so satisfying of like, here's over 100 million years of this spot on our planet, like this chunk of land on our planet, and everything that happened to it during that time.

343
01:01:04,552 --> 01:01:05,992
That's awesome.

344
01:01:06,245 --> 01:01:16,647
Yeah, well, and it really struck me as I was putting these notes together for this episode and as I was putting information together in the blog post that will be up after this episode is done.

345
01:01:17,327 --> 01:01:27,990
This really captured one of the, I've often said, one of my favorite things about paleontology, one of the reasons that I think geology is so cool, is that there's a story.

346
01:01:28,146 --> 01:01:57,873
Yes, it tells the story of what has happened over the history of the Earth. And what I found so satisfying and really interesting as I was putting these notes together is that there's a lot to talk about in, you know, the discovery of this region and the geography of the region and what specific deposits and things are present there, which we'll talk a bit about, but the part that I just did by itself is an extraordinary satisfying discussion.

347
01:01:57,909 --> 01:02:11,751
Just following the depositional history of this region, going through what happened, you could write a book, you could write a whole book that, and maybe somebody did, it wouldn't surprise me, that is just, here's the story of the Karu Supergroup.

348
01:02:12,069 --> 01:02:26,272
We start with glaciation and mountain building at the beginning of Pangaea, we follow through, there's tragedy, there's new things that come about, there's some familiar faces that first show up on the scene, there's more tragedy,

349
01:02:26,608 --> 01:02:52,554
and then it ends with a large igneous province that hails the end of Pangaea. That in and of itself is both super fun and exciting, that could have been the entire episode, and perfectly encapsulates one of the things that got me into paleontology and geology, the way that you can read the story of the Earth in these layers of rock.

350
01:02:53,249 --> 01:03:06,012
Absolutely. This is a really beautiful example of that concept, very nicely packaged into one region of Earth.

351
01:03:06,492 --> 01:03:16,254
Yes, and it's extra satisfying, I think, that it's a region of Earth that isn't the kind of region that most of our famous paleontology stories are from.

352
01:03:16,328 --> 01:03:37,134
There's lots of really awesome paleontology and archaeological history in Africa, and there are some famous sites down there. I'm very happy to get to highlight and spotlight this region in a continent that gets far less appreciation for paleontology than, for example, our continent, or the European continent.

353
01:03:37,589 --> 01:03:41,310
It is well deserved and very pleasing.

354
01:03:42,070 --> 01:03:53,793
Now, as I mentioned earlier, it would be completely unreasonable to try to describe the full extent of incredible fossil discoveries within the Karu Supergroup.

355
01:03:54,405 --> 01:04:09,069
So instead, I'm going to highlight some specific examples of fossil discoveries from across the Karu Supergroup to give just a sense of the diversity and variety of things that can be found there.

356
01:04:09,669 --> 01:04:16,491
After the break, we're going to go through just some of the coolest fossil discoveries that have ever been made in the world. It's so cool.

