01:04:16,787 --> 01:04:19,468
Stay tuned after this musical interlude for that.

358
01:04:33,387 --> 01:04:51,112
The remainder of this episode discussion is a section of my notes that I have titled "Notable Places and Cases." Some examples of exceptional Karu Supergroups sites and studies. We'll go through some particular examples. I'll also point out along the way, a bunch of these are things that we've talked about on the podcast before.

359
01:04:51,252 --> 01:04:57,253
Yeah, even though we haven't been announcing it every time, we have not been able to avoid the Karu Supergroup.

360
01:04:58,366 --> 01:05:15,631
Most of this list is pulled from a specific 2023 study by Amis Bordi et al. that set out specifically to highlight specific Karu sites of geoheritage significance in the area.

361
01:05:15,671 --> 01:05:20,712
So that is extremely helpful. It is also open access and you will find a link to it in the blog post.

362
01:05:21,167 --> 01:05:28,589
Thank you. So let's go through some examples from bottom to top because I love doing things in chronological order.

363
01:05:30,045 --> 01:05:52,472
First place to highlight, the Ganzfontein paleosurface. This is part of the Lower Beaufort Group, a mid-Permian around 262 million years old. This is a geologic surface of a formation from an ancient floodplain that is covered in trackways, footprints, tracks, trace fossils.

364
01:05:52,909 --> 01:06:03,372
It's about a thousand square meters of exposed surface originally discovered in the 1960s after heavy rains exposed the sediments.

365
01:06:04,152 --> 01:06:17,095
It is most famous for an extremely diverse set of therapsid trackways. Therapsid's part of that before mammals diversity of early synapsids.

366
01:06:17,347 --> 01:06:18,748
Man, you gotta love floodplains.

367
01:06:19,308 --> 01:06:33,092
They are fantastic. These include big five-toed footprints that probably belong to dinosophalians, which were very large synapsids, and paraeosaurs, which were early reptile relatives.

368
01:06:33,872 --> 01:06:39,214
There are smaller tracks that have been identified as temnospondyls, so early amphibians.

369
01:06:39,747 --> 01:06:49,790
There are also swimming traces, the sort of sinuous grooves that you get from the fins and tails of fish as they swim in very shallow water.

370
01:06:50,410 --> 01:07:00,433
Likely, according to research, from fish that were in pools as the pools receded, as they dried out and the water got very shallow.

371
01:07:00,907 --> 01:07:06,348
There are also little tiny trails left by arthropods, by bugs and stuff walking across this surface.

372
01:07:06,888 --> 01:07:20,572
So this is an area, we have these in a number of places in the world, where it's just a slab of rock covered in the comings and going traces of all sorts of animals that lived in that area.

373
01:07:20,612 --> 01:07:23,513
Walking, swimming, just moving across it.

374
01:07:23,664 --> 01:07:37,108
Well, it's like we talked about, not kinds of fossils and fossil evidence fossilize equally well in the same situations and sediments, and trace fossils need a particular set of situations to be able to be preserved.

375
01:07:37,168 --> 01:07:47,550
Like, footprints happen constantly, but most mud's not gonna preserve. So when you do have a large area that's that right condition, you can get a ridiculous amount.

376
01:07:48,107 --> 01:08:06,512
Yes. Also worth noting, a similar deposit that isn't the same paleo surface, it's not the same geologic formation, the Dave Green paleo surface, which is also in the Beaufort Group, also Permian within the Karu Supergroup,

377
01:08:06,850 --> 01:08:28,937
which we mentioned in the news in episode 163, because it featured, there was a paper that came out last year, describing traces and footprints and stuff from a large temnospondyl, ancient amphibian, that included several resting traces, which is the body impression, and swim trails between them.

378
01:08:29,649 --> 01:08:35,610
Which allowed them to interpret the crocodile-like swimming patterns of these ancient amphibians.

379
01:08:36,731 --> 01:08:37,791
So satisfying.

380
01:08:38,431 --> 01:08:54,374
Moving up the sequence a little bit, another great example of Karu deposits is at the Kitching Fossil Exploration Center, which is built near fossil deposits in the village of New Bethesda, on the banks of the Gatz River.

381
01:08:54,787 --> 01:09:09,412
This is also within the Beaufort Group, later Permian, so almost right at the end of the Permian, around 255 million years ago, which means that it records an ecosystem just before the Permian mass extinction.

382
01:09:09,527 --> 01:09:19,970
This is a snapshot of what did the world, or at least southern Africa, look like before the apocalypse, in the pre-apocalypse.

383
01:09:21,530 --> 01:09:28,972
Fossils of vertebrates have been being collected in this particular site since the early 1900s.

384
01:09:29,046 --> 01:09:42,890
There's a century of collection here, which includes early reptiles and para-reptiles, as well as Glossopteris and other plants, and the thing that these rocks are most famous for, tons of synapsids.

385
01:09:43,546 --> 01:10:05,331
Therapsids in particular, including Dicynodonts, things like and related to Lystrosaurus, Cynodonts, which are yet one family step closer to mammals, and Gorgonopsids, including one notable example that I've seen mentioned, Rubigia, which was among the largest Gorgonopsids known.

386
01:10:05,608 --> 01:10:14,270
This was a tiger-sized predator in the late Permian, which made it one of the largest land predators in the world at this time.

387
01:10:14,287 --> 01:10:38,793
-Awesome! -Continuing upwards, staying within the Beaufort group, but moving into the earliest part of the Triassic, so this time just after the Permian mass extinction, around 251 million years ago, a site that is called the Fairydale Lystrosaurus Graveyard, because sometimes we give fossil sites very evocative names.

388
01:10:38,813 --> 01:10:47,755
-Well, and also, like, immediately tells you what to expect. -Tells you what's there. It's Lystrosaurus, and none of them are happy.

389
01:10:47,810 --> 01:10:57,352
This is a fossil deposit that is at a farm site. That farm site, I think, has been called Fairydale. It's got another name that's, like, Donald, and then a number after it.

390
01:10:57,926 --> 01:11:10,850
At this deposit, research has identified over 500 skeletons of various tetrapod animals, including lots of Lystrosaurus.

391
01:11:11,206 --> 01:11:33,172
We've mentioned Lystrosaurus a number of times throughout the podcast history. Lystrosaurus was this sort of boar-shaped and boar-sized dicynodont, not a mammal, but not too far off from mammals, with tusks and a beak that is famous for doing extraordinarily well after the Permian mass extinction.

392
01:11:33,589 --> 01:11:44,691
Lystrosaurus is extremely widespread in the post-apocalyptic world. For whatever reason, it is the most common fossil animal at a lot of fossil deposits at this time.

393
01:11:46,329 --> 01:12:06,054
Lystrosaurus has been being collected at the Lystrosaurus graveyard since the 1970s. Alongside Lystrosaurus, there's also remnants of other synapses and other animals, including burrows that have been attributed to small synapses.

394
01:12:06,209 --> 01:12:20,333
synodonts in particular, and at one part in the site, there is a pair of Thrinaxodon, which is a small synodont that are an adult and a juvenile that have been interpreted as possibly a family couple.

395
01:12:21,874 --> 01:12:27,935
One of the few cases we have of potential evidence for parental care in synodonts.

396
01:12:28,406 --> 01:12:36,288
Very cool. I'm getting all the flashbacks from the Walking with Dinosaurs Triassic episode, so this is very neat.

397
01:12:36,968 --> 01:12:44,030
We have mentioned the Fairydale Lystrosaurus graveyard on the podcast before, although I don't know if we mentioned the site by name.

398
01:12:44,684 --> 01:13:07,810
There was a 2022 study that we talked about in the news in episode 151 that examined Lystrosaurus assemblages within this site and noted the repeated sequence of clusters of Lystrosaurus, where you'd get several skeletons all seemingly buried in the same place around the same time.

399
01:13:08,507 --> 01:13:23,991
All of which are juvenile or early adult, so relatively young, and which are often in these sort of spread eagle positions, which the authors interpreted as them having collapsed on the spot.

400
01:13:24,429 --> 01:13:49,094
They are buried alongside sediments from dried up river channels. The reason that this research was in the headlines and we talked about it is because these are interpreted as groups of Lystrosaurus that collapsed of starvation or thirst, and were dying relatively young because there wasn't a lot of available food or water.

401
01:13:49,691 --> 01:14:06,135
They point out in the paper that this is something that we see in herds today. During times of drought and stuff, you will get these sort of clusters of herding animals gathering around dwindling water sources, and then not all of them end up leaving, and so you get these bundles.

402
01:14:06,210 --> 01:14:18,433
This doesn't necessarily mean that Lystrosaurus was making herds like we think of today, but at the very least, they seem to have been gathering at these remnant water sources, and then not all of them survived.

403
01:14:18,473 --> 01:14:25,154
Yes, that they may have been all drawn to the same spot. They may not have gotten there together.

404
01:14:26,267 --> 01:14:41,451
This is an incredible snapshot of the world after the Permian extinction. There is evidence in various geologic bits of evidence for increasing aridity, drier conditions.

405
01:14:42,512 --> 01:14:50,154
We talk about Lystrosaurus as this incredible survivor, that they made it through the Permian extinction and then went on to be all over the world.

406
01:14:50,787 --> 01:15:00,350
One of the driving messages of this particular research and the evidence at this site is that it was a difficult time after the Permian extinction.

407
01:15:00,590 --> 01:15:08,032
Even for the species that were doing relatively well, this was a time where lots of animals were having a hard time surviving.

408
01:15:08,632 --> 01:15:15,194
Continuing on up sequence, still in the Beaufort group, because again, that's where a lot of the famous stuff is.

409
01:15:15,286 --> 01:15:26,591
Very shortly after this, still early Triassic around 250 million years old, the Lutzburg Pass burrow casts.

410
01:15:27,191 --> 01:15:37,995
These are road cuts that exposed sequences of sandstones and mudstones with burrow traces within them.

411
01:15:38,388 --> 01:15:43,150
So these are burrow casts, which means that this was a tunnel in the sediment that has since filled in.

412
01:15:43,750 --> 01:15:50,692
And that infill is what we're able to look at and go, "Ah, this is different from the sediment around it. This is a hole that got filled in."

413
01:15:51,092 --> 01:15:56,854
These burrows come in a variety of shapes. Some of them are branching and complex.

414
01:15:57,266 --> 01:16:06,330
At least one type of burrow there is spiral-shaped, kind of similar to the castoroids that we talked about in the rodents episode 203.

415
01:16:06,790 --> 01:16:12,712
These burrows have been attributed to various therapsids, early synapsids, synodonts, and such.

416
01:16:13,212 --> 01:16:20,915
They often have scratch marks in them from the animals that were presumably either digging the burrows or digging around in the burrows.

417
01:16:22,346 --> 01:16:30,309
Which is some of the oldest evidence that we have of burrowing behavior in these predecessors to mammals.

418
01:16:30,849 --> 01:16:31,749
Very neat.

419
01:16:32,409 --> 01:16:43,793
Also at this site, in addition to these synapsid burrows, there are also lungfish burrows. Today, lungfish do this. They will burrow into the sediment and sort of hunker down.

420
01:16:44,193 --> 01:16:47,054
There are burrows interpreted as lungfish burrows here as well.

421
01:16:47,426 --> 01:16:54,528
So cool. Once again, I also learned that from the Walking with Dinosaurs Triassic episode.

422
01:16:54,588 --> 01:17:02,611
This is one place that has burrows like this, but these kinds of burrow traces are actually quite common across Karu sediments.

423
01:17:02,631 --> 01:17:05,712
We see a lot of them in Triassic and Jurassic deposits.

424
01:17:06,226 --> 01:17:16,950
Continuing on upward in our partial tour of the supergroup, we'll move into the Jurassic to a site called the Moyeni Footprints in Lesotho.

425
01:17:17,510 --> 01:17:27,034
These are two well-documented trackway surfaces. So again, these are surfaces of rock formations with trackways within them.

426
01:17:27,487 --> 01:17:32,529
These are exposed near the town of Moyeni, hence the Moyeni Footprints in Lesotho.

427
01:17:32,969 --> 01:17:41,311
Both of them are within the Elliott Formation, which is early Jurassic around 195 and 190 million years old.

428
01:17:41,972 --> 01:17:49,154
The older of the two track sites preserves around 200 footprints preserved along an ancient riverbank

429
01:17:49,628 --> 01:17:58,291
that are attributed to early crocodyliformes, temnospondyls, various dinosaurs, as well as invertebrates.

430
01:17:58,331 --> 01:18:07,054
There's also microbial mat deposits in here, tons of trace fossils, just a whole ecosystem of things scooting along the ground.

431
01:18:07,467 --> 01:18:15,009
This was first documented back in the 1960s and 70s. These days, it is protected by the Dinosaur Footprints Visitors Center.

432
01:18:15,369 --> 01:18:19,971
So this is a place that if you happen to be in Lesotho, you can go visit and check this out.

433
01:18:20,451 --> 01:18:28,833
This deposit is famous because there's lots of cool behavioral signs that have been documented among these footprints.

434
01:18:29,266 --> 01:18:40,730
For a couple examples that were both mentioned in that 2023 paper, there's one Ornithischian dinosaur track. So these are the early big branch of herbivorous dinosaurs.

435
01:18:41,230 --> 01:18:47,332
There's one sequence of footprints that starts out quadrupedal and transitions to bipedal.

436
01:18:47,632 --> 01:18:48,252
Oh yeah!

437
01:18:48,392 --> 01:18:53,434
That it was walking on all fours and then stood up to walk on just the hind legs.

438
01:18:53,964 --> 01:18:59,906
And then there's another one, at least one, of amphibian tracks that show a belly drag.

439
01:19:00,827 --> 01:19:08,770
That it was not only walking, but scooting itself along the sediment the way that salamanders and crocs will do today.

440
01:19:08,830 --> 01:19:09,670
The belly crawl.

441
01:19:09,865 --> 01:19:22,451
Yes. The younger of the Mjölnir track sites notably preserves around 50 tracks with three toes that are the very classic theropod dinosaur footprints.

442
01:19:23,111 --> 01:19:30,714
The largest of these has been estimated to be from a dinosaur that was probably seven to eight meters long.

443
01:19:30,968 --> 01:19:40,271
Okay. So going on, you know, 25, 30 feet long, making them among the largest theropods and the largest predators of this time period.

444
01:19:40,331 --> 01:19:41,051
Yeah, that's not nothing.

445
01:19:41,071 --> 01:19:49,154
In the early Jurassic. So we've got trace fossil evidence of some of the world's largest predators from this time.

446
01:19:49,605 --> 01:20:03,751
That's so awesome. The footprints that go from four footed to two footed is... that could very likely be up at the top of my favorite fossil footprints ever.

447
01:20:03,811 --> 01:20:05,572
That's a very cool thing to get to see.

448
01:20:05,972 --> 01:20:06,293
Mm-hmm.

449
01:20:06,883 --> 01:20:17,008
Well, if you like footprints, I've got more. Beyond the Mjölnir footprints, the Elliot formation itself, this early Jurassic formation, preserves tons of really cool footprints.

450
01:20:17,668 --> 01:20:20,790
Two of which have been mentioned elsewhere in the podcast history.

451
01:20:21,206 --> 01:20:32,210
In episode 180, we talked about a bit of new research describing bird-like footprints from across the Elliot formation, from various different parts of this geologic formation,

452
01:20:32,731 --> 01:20:39,893
that were identified as probably early dinosaurs, but surprisingly bird-like feet.

453
01:20:40,173 --> 01:20:45,235
Not just like kind of bird-like, like all theropod feet, but very bird-like.

454
01:20:45,586 --> 01:20:50,928
And I remember the headlines were, you know, the discussions in the paper and in the headlines were very much like,

455
01:20:51,309 --> 01:20:56,111
"What was leaving bird footprints 30 million years before birds evolved?"

456
01:20:56,211 --> 01:20:56,411
Yep.

457
01:20:57,151 --> 01:21:05,194
And seems to be that bird-like feet were a thing some theropods evolved before actual birds had feet like that.

458
01:21:05,524 --> 01:21:10,967
Yeah, not time-traveling birds as might seem the obvious conclusion.

459
01:21:11,687 --> 01:21:16,649
Another thing, and this is one that I forgot, I didn't remember this until I saw it,

460
01:21:17,029 --> 01:21:26,273
the Elliot formation, as well as other formations from the early Jurassic in the Karu region, features trackways of hopping animals.

461
01:21:26,613 --> 01:21:27,553
Oh right, yeah.

462
01:21:27,585 --> 01:21:36,249
Which we talked about in episode 193, these tracks of quadrupedal and bipedal hopping across these sediments.

463
01:21:37,129 --> 01:21:43,512
So much fun. Oh, this is just all the footprints you could ever ask for.

464
01:21:44,052 --> 01:21:52,215
Absolutely. And finally, one more place to highlight, also in the early Jurassic 190 million years ago or so,

465
01:21:52,687 --> 01:22:00,070
the Roydry Dinosaur Nesting Grounds. These are located in Golden Gate Highlands National Park.

466
01:22:00,310 --> 01:22:02,631
These are in the upper part of the Elliot formation.

467
01:22:03,331 --> 01:22:09,714
As the name suggests, this deposit is famous for dinosaur nests.

468
01:22:10,125 --> 01:22:17,888
Dinosaur eggs were first documented at this locality back in the 1970s, and not only were there dinosaur eggs,

469
01:22:17,908 --> 01:22:22,911
which of course is a very exciting thing to find, but embryos inside the eggs.

470
01:22:23,471 --> 01:22:32,034
Which I don't know, I feel like we've said this specifically, but eggs are a well-known kind of trace fossil that we find.

471
01:22:32,515 --> 01:22:35,016
Typically there's nothing recognizable inside them.

472
01:22:35,086 --> 01:22:35,326
Yes.

473
01:22:35,806 --> 01:22:42,609
Either the embryos were not developed enough for anything to be fossilizable, that nothing was hardened up,

474
01:22:43,169 --> 01:22:45,950
or they are hatched or broken and emptied out.

475
01:22:46,290 --> 01:22:46,471
Yes.

476
01:22:46,851 --> 01:22:52,453
And so typically we don't, it may seem like one of those, well yeah, there's got to be a baby inside an egg.

477
01:22:52,533 --> 01:22:53,473
That's what's inside an egg.

478
01:22:54,384 --> 01:23:02,868
Usually, and even if you were to x-ray a chicken egg, a lot of the time you wouldn't get a lot from it unless you do it at the right stages.

479
01:23:03,248 --> 01:23:07,330
A lot of dinosaur, like fossil eggs, are just filled with sediment and minerals.

480
01:23:07,610 --> 01:23:11,852
So when we mention that they have embryos inside that, that's huge.

481
01:23:11,912 --> 01:23:12,532
That's special.

482
01:23:12,633 --> 01:23:18,835
That's a big deal. It's why it makes the news almost every time there's a study on embryos inside dino eggs.

483
01:23:18,864 --> 01:23:22,146
Yes, and these were some of the first discoveries of these.

484
01:23:22,326 --> 01:23:27,228
I probably remember this because I remember getting National Geographic's freaking out about it.

485
01:23:27,688 --> 01:23:31,309
And I think they were from Africa, so probably with some of these.

486
01:23:31,670 --> 01:23:32,890
Very well could have been.

487
01:23:33,310 --> 01:23:40,133
These eggs and embryos were identified as belonging to Massospondylus, which is one of those early, early dinosaur names that I know.

488
01:23:40,153 --> 01:23:42,414
It's stuck in my head because it comes up a lot.

489
01:23:42,805 --> 01:23:49,247
Massospondylus was a prosauropod, so within the group that gave rise eventually to the long-necked sauropods.

490
01:23:49,727 --> 01:23:54,409
Continued excavations, and I wouldn't be surprised if this was some of what was in your National Geographic,

491
01:23:54,649 --> 01:24:03,313
would uncover around a dozen nests in this location, closely spaced, each with up to 30 eggs in them,

492
01:24:04,306 --> 01:24:10,288
with evidence that this site was probably being visited for nesting year after year.

493
01:24:10,308 --> 01:24:20,452
That this appears to have been a colonial nesting ground where these dinosaurs came together, gathered to make a community nest.

494
01:24:20,892 --> 01:24:28,475
Which is something that we see actually in a lot of birds that will reuse the same nest.

495
01:24:28,848 --> 01:24:33,869
Nesting sites is very famous, but also singular nests a lot of the time.

496
01:24:34,510 --> 01:24:39,952
I would be fascinated to know with these dinosaurs, did everyone have their own nest?

497
01:24:40,172 --> 01:24:45,153
Or was it just this is the good place to nest? If there's a previous nest, feel free to move in.

498
01:24:45,353 --> 01:24:47,274
Or did you make them every year?

499
01:24:47,474 --> 01:24:52,596
You see this a lot with shore birds.

500
01:24:52,704 --> 01:24:56,105
Shore nesting and cliff nesting birds will often return to the same place.

501
01:24:56,766 --> 01:25:02,448
Well, because birds can do what most animals can't do, and they can just go nest on an island that nothing else can get to.

502
01:25:02,508 --> 01:25:08,290
Yeah, it's like bald eagles get to use their same nest every year because it's at the very top of a tree.

503
01:25:08,791 --> 01:25:10,371
So no one can do much to it.

504
01:25:10,391 --> 01:25:15,834
Yeah, birds from all over the place gather onto the same island and go, "Haha, no predators!"

505
01:25:16,165 --> 01:25:19,166
So we all have our babies here and then we fly away.

506
01:25:20,227 --> 01:25:29,391
These dinosaur nests are among the world's oldest evidence for dinosaur communal nesting and some of the oldest dinosaur embryos in the world.

507
01:25:30,031 --> 01:25:37,154
So awesome. These days, that area is also the location of the Columelumo Dinosaur Center.

508
01:25:37,588 --> 01:25:43,110
So this is another place that has been turned into not only a research site, but an education site.

509
01:25:43,370 --> 01:25:47,331
It's so satisfying that fossil sites for research, awesome stuff.

510
01:25:47,732 --> 01:25:52,033
It's also very cool when the public gets to participate.

511
01:25:52,784 --> 01:26:04,108
When centers and buildings are made like this that invite people into these amazing places that usually are so disconnected from...

512
01:26:04,729 --> 01:26:08,490
Even when we explain where Velociraptor was dug up...

513
01:26:08,510 --> 01:26:09,410
Somewhere around the desert.

514
01:26:09,590 --> 01:26:16,253
It's still this abstract somewhere in the dirt, somewhere in Mongolia, or somewhere in the Midwest of the United States.

515
01:26:17,105 --> 01:26:23,107
Getting to actually see one of these places is very cool, so it's satisfying that there's so many in the Karu Supergroup.

516
01:26:23,408 --> 01:26:34,452
Well, when you find a space like that that has such an incredible deposit of fossils and is such an incredible resource of information, you either have to just leave it there.

517
01:26:34,812 --> 01:26:37,674
If it's out in the middle of nowhere, you can't do anything with it.

518
01:26:37,994 --> 01:26:40,495
That's one of the reasons why a lot of fossil sites...

519
01:26:40,563 --> 01:26:47,326
And I don't know off the top of my head of any specific examples within the Karu Supergroup, although I wouldn't be surprised at all if there are some...

520
01:26:47,826 --> 01:26:50,887
Of fossil sites whose location isn't made public.

521
01:26:50,947 --> 01:26:51,688
Yes, exactly.

522
01:26:51,708 --> 01:27:00,851
And you'll see in a paper sometimes they'll say, "This is the general region of where this deposit is, but we're not actually going to tell you the specific location."

523
01:27:01,311 --> 01:27:05,313
And the reason that sometimes scientists will do that is to keep it safe.

524
01:27:05,426 --> 01:27:14,569
Because if we publish the location and we say, "There's 100 dinosaur eggs there," there's a market for stolen fossils.

525
01:27:15,149 --> 01:27:21,091
And there's a good chance people will go out there and swipe those fossils, and then they won't be available for research next year.

526
01:27:21,327 --> 01:27:35,833
And even if we don't get a bunch of people ransacking the site, it makes me think of those posts I've seen of especially picturesque locales that get viral, that go viral on TikTok or something.

527
01:27:36,213 --> 01:27:43,415
And then you'll see people posting of like, "We went to that one canyon from that one viral video, and it's just full of people."

528
01:27:43,806 --> 01:27:47,547
Because everyone else also saw it, and a bunch of them decided to go there.

529
01:27:48,027 --> 01:27:53,088
So it's also just like-- Bringing extra attention to it can get in the way of the research you're trying to do.

530
01:27:53,108 --> 01:27:58,310
Yeah, crowds do not typically do well to preserving an area.

531
01:27:58,630 --> 01:28:07,372
Whether they're there to take stuff or just there to take pictures, that's still a lot of feet, that's still a lot of people who may or may not be making the best decisions.

532
01:28:07,946 --> 01:28:24,931
And like I said, a lot of these and more places across the Karu Supergroup are legally protected, they are preserved by the local governments, law enforcement, and a lot of them have been made into research and education centers.

533
01:28:25,008 --> 01:28:38,911
Which is something that, when you have the resources to do it, is an even better alternative to protecting it than not sharing the location, is putting a building on it and fences around it and then inviting people to come see it now that it's safe and secure.

534
01:28:39,011 --> 01:28:47,914
Just building walls and a roof around and over a section of it or as much of it as you can, which is such a cool thing that we can do.

535
01:28:48,227 --> 01:28:52,269
Like, so glad we invented buildings.

536
01:28:54,449 --> 01:29:09,794
One of the coolest things about the Karu Supergroup is, in addition to being an incredible resource for research, in addition to being just an amazing chunk of the story of Earth history, and on top of being a great resource for education and tourism,

537
01:29:10,548 --> 01:29:20,271
it is also often highlighted as a really significant example of the region's geo-heritage or natural heritage.

538
01:29:20,651 --> 01:29:30,314
This is something that we've talked about here and there on the podcast before that is something that gets, hopefully, more and more attention, is getting more attention over time.

539
01:29:30,747 --> 01:29:40,150
The idea that these kinds of localities aren't just interesting scientifically, they're interesting because they're part of a place where people live.

540
01:29:40,970 --> 01:29:49,652
This is something that you can have a level of connection to and a level of relation to because this is part of your geo-heritage.

541
01:29:50,126 --> 01:30:03,650
For people that live in the southern portion of Africa, in these countries that the Karu Supergroup sits underneath, this is something that that region has that nowhere else in the world gets.

542
01:30:04,190 --> 01:30:13,753
These are fossils that are only found here in your part of the world. These are scientific discoveries that are only made here in this part of the world.

543
01:30:14,288 --> 01:30:27,293
And that, not only can that stimulate interest in the science, not only can that stimulate economic growth, if you're proud of it, you're putting funding into it, you're building these museums and education centers,

544
01:30:27,607 --> 01:30:38,710
but it's also just something, an additional bit of local pride that you and your community get to have that these super cool fossils are from the place that you live.

545
01:30:39,090 --> 01:30:49,292
It's the exact same as people who feel a connection to the mountains that they grew up around, or the river that they grew up near, or the animals.

546
01:30:49,348 --> 01:31:03,132
And we talk about people taking pride in, we're the only place that has the American alligator, it's one of two alligators on the planet, and in all the states alligators are found, people are proud of their gators.

547
01:31:03,307 --> 01:31:17,111
It's the same concept of, you have a heritage, a part of Earth's history, recorded in a specific way that is only recorded that way here, with fossils, many of which are only found there.

548
01:31:17,891 --> 01:31:25,074
The paper, like I said, a lot of the descriptions that I just took of these sites came out of this 2023 Borde et al. paper,

549
01:31:25,667 --> 01:31:31,929
and I think that they sum it up very succinctly, I think the sentence is either from the abstract or the conclusion, where they say,

550
01:31:32,389 --> 01:31:45,813
"Here we present six Karu sites of global geoscientific importance that best display that heritage, with the caveat that these sites only touch upon the Karu riches that are available for academic research

551
01:31:46,113 --> 01:31:49,354
and the emerging paleotourism industry."

552
01:31:49,627 --> 01:31:54,968
The Karu Supergroup is full of fascinating discoveries waiting to be made.

553
01:31:55,528 --> 01:32:05,652
It's such a cool place to get to highlight and talk about, it will for sure continue to show up in episodes in the news, in the main discussions, because you can't not.

554
01:32:06,172 --> 01:32:10,593
You can only talk about paleontology for so long before you are in the Karu Supergroup.

555
01:32:11,527 --> 01:32:21,671
It is inevitable. Oh, such a satisfying, like, the Karu Supergroup just scratches all the paleo itches.

556
01:32:22,172 --> 01:32:28,914
It's got something for everybody. That's not true, there's no snakes there. There's also technically no crocs in there.

557
01:32:29,027 --> 01:32:38,270
Almost, almost perfect. If only there was like a Cretaceous formation, just a little bit, just a little bit of Cretaceous formation on top of it, we could get crocs and snakes.

558
01:32:38,290 --> 01:32:41,791
We'd be good, we'd be good. That would be it.

559
01:32:42,031 --> 01:32:46,773
That's why the Karu Supergroup has to overcompensate so much with all this other awesome stuff.

560
01:32:46,833 --> 01:32:50,674
I was going to go the other, the Karu Supergroup, it can only be so awesome.

561
01:32:50,854 --> 01:32:53,975
It would be too much. That would be too much. We wouldn't be able to handle it.

562
01:32:53,985 --> 01:32:59,487
The continent would break apart again. There would just be too much awesome stuff weighing it down.

563
01:32:59,527 --> 01:33:04,970
That section of rock layer would just leave Earth, it just would be too good for us.

564
01:33:06,571 --> 01:33:14,234
Check out the blog post. I'm going to put in a bunch of pictures of some of the stuff we've been talking about, a link to that paper and others.

565
01:33:14,527 --> 01:33:23,770
And also, for fun, a link to a bunch of times that we have talked about the Karu Supergroup fossils in the news, which is very satisfying.

566
01:33:23,950 --> 01:33:24,671
Yay!

567
01:33:24,684 --> 01:33:42,309
Hey, before we wrap everything up officially, there is one thing left to do, and that is patron questions. One of the benefits that our patrons can get in return for their fantastic generous support of us on our Patreon is the ability to submit questions for us to ask here at the end of a podcast.

568
01:33:42,949 --> 01:33:47,011
This episode, we have two questions.

569
01:33:47,171 --> 01:33:47,451
Dos.

570
01:33:48,004 --> 01:33:50,325
Well, tell us the questions.

571
01:33:50,963 --> 01:34:15,356
First question is from Jackie. "I know the KPG boundary is an identifiable layer in the rocks. In some places, do other era transitions have an identifiable layer, or do any at least have a site with somewhat contiguous records through the transition where we can study the extinction/turnover of species?"

572
01:34:16,030 --> 01:34:20,812
Excellent question. Partially answered by the discussion that we had here in this episode.

573
01:34:20,912 --> 01:34:21,392
Yes, indeed.

574
01:34:21,464 --> 01:34:42,553
Yeah, we do in many places actually get sites that span across a geological boundary. The end of the Cretaceous is kind of unique because an event happened at the end of the Cretaceous that put down a very specific, distinctive, and identifiable thin layer of sediment.

575
01:34:42,673 --> 01:34:42,893
Yes.

576
01:34:43,533 --> 01:34:46,775
The visible KPG boundary is asteroid dust.

577
01:34:46,823 --> 01:34:54,106
Yeah, something was added to Earth's geology that signified that event.

578
01:34:54,447 --> 01:34:57,928
That is very rare. That's actually very rare even for the Late Cretaceous.

579
01:34:58,028 --> 01:34:58,528
Yes.

580
01:34:58,568 --> 01:35:03,030
That asteroid dust layer is only actually preserved in a handful of places.

581
01:35:03,371 --> 01:35:07,092
There's not a shell of that layer around the Earth.

582
01:35:07,412 --> 01:35:09,133
Yes, at least not one that's preserved.

583
01:35:09,173 --> 01:35:09,934
Yes, exactly.

584
01:35:10,562 --> 01:35:17,225
Often the boundary between two geologic time periods isn't quite as clear as that.

585
01:35:17,746 --> 01:35:22,328
In many places where the boundary is visible, that's because something's missing in between them.

586
01:35:22,468 --> 01:35:22,648
Yes.

587
01:35:23,064 --> 01:35:38,633
Where you had deposition in, say, the Jurassic, and then some erosional event, and then further deposition within the Cretaceous, and enough time passed between them that the environment had changed and the sediments are different.

588
01:35:38,953 --> 01:35:48,558
So you can say this is the line between the geologic periods, but that line wasn't there when it happened.

589
01:35:48,589 --> 01:35:48,809
Yes.

590
01:35:49,029 --> 01:35:50,750
You're missing some pieces in between.

591
01:35:51,170 --> 01:35:51,790
Absolutely.

592
01:35:52,190 --> 01:35:59,193
But, as in the Karu supergroup, from top to bottom spans four different periods of geologic time.

593
01:35:59,833 --> 01:36:07,235
To my knowledge, in most of those places, it isn't as clean as just saying there's the Permian, there's the Triassic right above it.

594
01:36:07,886 --> 01:36:15,788
Usually there's a bit of a gradation, usually we have to look very closely and say, "Here's where the fossils change over."

595
01:36:16,168 --> 01:36:19,549
We often have to go, "If you see this fossil, it's this period."

596
01:36:19,609 --> 01:36:19,829
Yes.

597
01:36:20,409 --> 01:36:25,031
And that is where your official boundary happens between them.

598
01:36:25,564 --> 01:36:38,870
But if you have just a continuous deposition, you often can't see the line between them, or you don't have super continuous deposition, in which case there's little gaps that separate one time period from the other.

599
01:36:39,027 --> 01:36:54,312
And so, once again, you'll get what are effectively distinct in the beginning points, because you've cut out a part, so you've broken the gradation into distinct end pieces.

600
01:36:54,572 --> 01:36:58,193
And even the Karu supergroup is largely uninterrupted.

601
01:36:58,213 --> 01:36:58,373
Yeah.

602
01:36:58,565 --> 01:37:03,669
But it itself is still not a perfect day-by-day deposition over 100 million years.

603
01:37:03,689 --> 01:37:10,093
Not every year of that 100 million years is preserved.

604
01:37:10,627 --> 01:37:16,770
And often, even if we do have that record across a boundary, we don't always have the resolution to be able to see it very clearly.

605
01:37:16,910 --> 01:37:17,110
Yes.

606
01:37:17,450 --> 01:37:21,812
Again, the KPG, those handful of KPG sites are very unusual in that regard.

607
01:37:22,533 --> 01:37:25,834
Great question. Very suitable for this episode.

608
01:37:25,854 --> 01:37:27,195
Let's do another one.

609
01:37:27,703 --> 01:37:29,364
Next up is from Nancy.

610
01:37:29,384 --> 01:37:36,847
"The Cenozoic by itself is as long as any named period from the Mesozoic or Paleozoic.

611
01:37:36,867 --> 01:37:50,513
Is this purely a function of our more granular view of it, as in more fossils, etc., or is evolution faster now than it was in, let's say, the Ordovician, Permian, or Jurassic?"

612
01:37:50,953 --> 01:37:52,574
That is a very interesting question.

613
01:37:53,288 --> 01:38:02,131
We've talked about this many times on the podcast, that we always break down geologic time periods into smaller and smaller chunks.

614
01:38:02,531 --> 01:38:06,333
And those smaller chunks get finer and finer as you get closer to the present.

615
01:38:06,433 --> 01:38:06,613
Yes.

616
01:38:06,793 --> 01:38:08,514
Because there's more evidence still.

617
01:38:08,574 --> 01:38:11,935
The longer it, the older it is, the more time there's been for us to lose evidence.

618
01:38:12,423 --> 01:38:18,585
Nancy is pointing out that the Cenozoic is about 66 million years old from beginning to end.

619
01:38:19,085 --> 01:38:22,627
The Cretaceous period by itself is like 80 million years.

620
01:38:23,047 --> 01:38:23,227
Yep.

621
01:38:23,727 --> 01:38:29,009
So there is this interesting... the durations are not consistent.

622
01:38:29,129 --> 01:38:33,311
Yeah, that not all periods and epochs are made equal.

623
01:38:33,945 --> 01:38:51,072
Yes. To my knowledge, there isn't any evidence or reason to suspect that change happens more quickly over time, or that evolution is making shifts and changes faster as we get closer to the present.

624
01:38:51,553 --> 01:38:56,955
It is much more because we have more evidence that we're able to break things up more finely.

625
01:38:56,995 --> 01:38:57,215
Yes.

626
01:38:57,925 --> 01:39:06,689
It is worth keeping in mind on top of that, you know, we do split other time period... like the Cretaceous is split into several ages within it.

627
01:39:06,929 --> 01:39:07,209
Absolutely.

628
01:39:07,229 --> 01:39:10,791
And then those themselves are split into periods within them.

629
01:39:11,171 --> 01:39:17,034
We don't call... the names are different. We call those periods and then ages or stages or whatever's within them.

630
01:39:17,505 --> 01:39:25,808
It is also worth keeping in mind that a lot of the major names that we are aware of these days were named quite a while ago.

631
01:39:25,928 --> 01:39:26,128
Yes.

632
01:39:26,668 --> 01:39:38,652
That the Paleozoic, Mesozoic, Cenozoic is a distinction that was made quite a long time ago, looking at a pretty broad view of the fossil record.

633
01:39:38,692 --> 01:39:40,733
Mostly, I believe, from marine sediments.

634
01:39:42,286 --> 01:39:56,311
So the way the geologic timescale is broken down in those bigger chunks that we typically end up talking about, the eras and the periods, is itself a relic of much earlier paleontological research where they had far less resolution.

635
01:39:56,352 --> 01:39:56,552
Yes.

636
01:39:57,246 --> 01:40:13,591
It makes me think of the very like almost one to one comparable scenario of how we discuss recent and more distant human history, where it's like the past century we discussed by the decade of the 80s, the 20s.

637
01:40:14,371 --> 01:40:18,193
Then as you go further back, you start discussing things by like the century.

638
01:40:18,633 --> 01:40:18,893
Yes.

639
01:40:18,984 --> 01:40:28,449
So you're doing the 1700s. During the 1700s, here's what was happening. There'll be specific dates that come up, but we'll often kind of generalize it.

640
01:40:28,469 --> 01:40:39,015
Then you go back to like ancient Rome and ancient Egypt, and we'll be talking about multiple centuries at a time of like, yeah, between 400 and 600 or blah, blah, blah, this was happening.

641
01:40:39,589 --> 01:40:45,971
But like when we talk about the Dark Ages, they were still having decade by decade shifts and changes.

642
01:40:45,991 --> 01:40:46,431
Oh, sure.

643
01:40:46,671 --> 01:40:52,274
They were happening across nations and within nations, but we've lost a lot of that info.

644
01:40:52,874 --> 01:40:56,435
And for the general discussion, we sum it up.

645
01:40:56,895 --> 01:40:57,095
Yes.

646
01:40:57,203 --> 01:41:04,487
So we can't do a what were the 20s like every single century because we may not know for all examples.

647
01:41:05,028 --> 01:41:06,709
We've lost some of that information.

648
01:41:07,169 --> 01:41:18,775
Yeah. And a lot of the main geologic period sort of boundaries are set by some of the most significant and most obvious changes that have happened in the history of our planet.

649
01:41:19,286 --> 01:41:24,128
Right. The broad scale, just the periods and eras, most of that mass extinction.

650
01:41:24,168 --> 01:41:24,868
Yes.

651
01:41:24,948 --> 01:41:28,650
It's the stuff that you can see much more clearly in the record.

652
01:41:29,050 --> 01:41:30,130
That it's hard to miss.

653
01:41:30,330 --> 01:41:39,254
It's hard to miss. They're not evenly spaced out. They're sort of scattered around, but then we still get more resolution.

654
01:41:39,286 --> 01:41:52,252
You can see the fluctuating glacial periods of the Ice Age in many cases, just as obviously and easily as you can see where the Permian mass extinction happened and separated those two.

655
01:41:52,572 --> 01:41:52,793
Yes.

656
01:41:53,353 --> 01:41:58,996
So we've always had better resolution closer to the present that affects how we name things.

657
01:41:59,384 --> 01:42:05,267
But at the end of the day, really the shortest and quickest answer is it's all very much arbitrary.

658
01:42:05,387 --> 01:42:05,547
Yep.

659
01:42:05,947 --> 01:42:11,690
Where we draw the lines, where we name them, it was arbitrary 100 years ago. It continues to be arbitrary today.

660
01:42:12,110 --> 01:42:17,012
Two periods that are called periods are not necessarily comparable.

661
01:42:17,365 --> 01:42:29,189
The same way that families of organisms, like the family hominidae, isn't the same thing as the family Canidae or Felidae or Tyrannosauridae.

662
01:42:29,569 --> 01:42:31,810
It's just a category that we've come up with.

663
01:42:32,065 --> 01:42:54,432
And I think my favorite example that proves that point that has come up multiple times on Ali's episodes is if we were to have named these time periods based off of the plant fossil record, we'd probably have entirely different, not just segments, but major segments of breaking up Earth's history.

664
01:42:54,472 --> 01:42:57,894
It might not be the Paleozoic, Mesozoic, and Cenozoic.

665
01:42:57,929 --> 01:43:04,711
We might be broken in half in the water, and then when they finally came on land, and then we break it up. Like, who knows?

666
01:43:05,591 --> 01:43:15,595
There's a lot of criteria we could have used, and we're building off of what was used and have continued to refine it in various ways.

667
01:43:16,055 --> 01:43:19,456
I guess it also might be worth pointing out that the Cenozoic isn't over.

668
01:43:19,696 --> 01:43:20,376
Yes, exactly.

669
01:43:20,383 --> 01:43:26,026
The Cenozoic is short because there hasn't been another shift major enough for us to decide to end it.

670
01:43:26,466 --> 01:43:30,268
It's the same way as when you look at the epochs of the Cenozoic.

671
01:43:30,788 --> 01:43:36,891
The last epoch on the list is the Holocene, which is only like 10,000 years old.

672
01:43:37,451 --> 01:43:40,552
Because that's now. That's right now. That's still happening.

673
01:43:41,225 --> 01:43:48,947
If geologists were looking at this from 2 million years in the future, the Holocene might also be a million or 2 million years long.

674
01:43:49,087 --> 01:43:55,149
Exactly. Like, we haven't had as much time since the Mesozoic as there was in the Mesozoic.

675
01:43:55,410 --> 01:43:58,331
Yes. So yeah, it's also skewing things.

